      </arrangement>
      <bioghist id="aspace_d4e04ad8a69e812aa0d49bf5cdf342c0">
         <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
         <p>John Britton was a prolific topographer, writer, and editor in the early nineteenth century.  Britton was born 1 July 1771 in Wiltshire, England, to Henry Britton and Anne Hillier.  John was one of ten children.  Britton became interested in topography in 1789 following a meeting with his future collaborator Edward Wedlake Brayley.  Together, they worked with the publisher Vernon and Hood to undertake a topographical survey of England and Wales.  Brayley and Britton traveled primarily on foot, and the extensive traveling gave Britton an interest in English antiquities.  It also lead to a new interest in architecture.  Antiquities and architecture became a part of John Britton's publishing career, dovetailing with his established interest in topography.  He authored a large number of books that combined all three, such as <title>
               <part>The Beauties of England and Wales</part>
            </title> authored with Brayley and published between 1801 and 1814, <title>
               <part>Architectural Antiquities of Great Britain</part>
            </title>  and <title>
               <part>Cathedral Antiquities of England</part>
            </title>.  He edited many more.</p>
         <p>Informed by his interest in antiquities, Britton actively campaigned for government protection of monuments in the first half of the nineteenth century.  He was also an advisor to the Royal Literary Fund, part of the Art Union Committee, and received a civil-list pension from then Chancellor of the Exchequer Benjamin Disraeli.  Britton married Mary Rule (1772-1848) in 1803 and then Ellen Morgan in 1849.  No children came from either marriage.  John Britton died of bronchitis at his home in St Pancras London on January 1, 1857.</p>
      </bioghist>
